      "summary": {
        "en": {
          "research_question": "How do national advisory organizations differ in their policy recommendations for administering the Qdenga dengue vaccine to international travelers?",
          "study_design": "A comparative policy analysis that evaluated publicly accessible national guidelines alongside survey responses from health advisory committees.",
          "population_setting": "Public health advisory bodies representing seventeen nations, primarily non-endemic regions where the vaccine holds formal licensing approval.",
          "main_findings": "National directives consistently exceeded regulatory permissions in restrictiveness while demonstrating uniform support for individuals with confirmed past infections. Eligibility criteria for travelers lacking documented infection history showed substantial cross-border variation based on inconsistent application of exposure metrics and host susceptibility factors. Protocols also diverged regarding infection verification methods and pre-departure dosing schedules.",
          "public_health_relevance": "Inconsistent international protocols complicate clinical counseling and traveler preparedness for dengue-endemic destinations. Harmonizing future advisories will likely depend on supplementary effectiveness data targeting specific demographic groups currently underrepresented in clinical trials. Maintaining transparent dialogue between providers and patients remains critical amid these policy discrepancies.",
          "limitations": "The investigation synthesizes existing policy documents and committee surveys rather than measuring direct clinical outcomes or population-level vaccination coverage. Interpretive disparities stem from inherent differences between controlled trial cohorts and real-world travel demographics.",
          "gids_interpretation": "The manuscript demonstrates how identical foundational research can produce divergent regulatory pathways when expert panels navigate evidentiary gaps differently. It provides context for understanding guideline heterogeneity without implying changes in underlying pathogen circulation or novel safety signals.",
          "provenance": {
            "generated_by": "literature-evidence-agent",
            "model": "qwen3.7-flash",
            "provider": "qwenai",
            "quality_score": 0.936,
            "generated_at": "2026-08-13T12:41:39.630422+00:00",
